==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|timous}}

===Etymology===
From earlier {{m|en|timous}}, from {{inh|en|enm|tymous}}, *{{m|enm||tymwos, *tymwis|early}}, equivalent to {{m|en|timewise}}. Cognate with {{cog|sco|timeous||timely}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈtaɪməs/}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# {{lb|en|especially|Scotland|South Africa}}<!--EDD says also Ireland--> In [[sufficient]] [[time]]; [[timely]]; in (its) proper time or season.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1831|title=The Radical Reformer's Currycomb; Or, Scrub for Scrub|page=2
|text=We sincerely hope, however, that a '''timeous''' and speedy adoption of energetic and conciliatory measures will waft such a dreadful calamity far distant from our homes, and restore that confidence in society so necessary for the welfare of a commercial community.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1919|title=The Scottish Law Review and Sheriff Court Reports|page=93
|text=Now, the pursuer avers that he sent '''timeous''' notice, in terms of the conditions, to the defender, the seller. Be it observed that the words are that the objection must be intimated to the auctioneers not later than 7 p.m. on the following Friday, not that the intimation must be ''received'' by them not later than that hour.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|opportune}}, {{l|en|seasonable}}; see also [[Thesaurus:timely]]

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|timeously}}
* {{l|en|untimeous}}, {{l|en|untimous}}

